  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through next Friday)\nIssued at 1142 PM CDT FRI AUG 7 2026\n\nCentral Alabama remains in a somewhat weak upper flow area south \nof the main broad trough that extends out of Central Canada and \ndown into the Great Lakes. At the same time, Alabama is situated \nbetween two ridges, one across the Four Corners and the other in \nthe Western Atlantic off of the FL Coast. The difference from \nyesterday is that guidance is now showing a small developing \nshortwave moving in from the northwest. While this will not make a\nhuge difference, it should orient our highest rain chances to the\nnorthwest/north areas for Sat/Sun. Our emphasis will still be \ntypical summertime diurnally enhanced convection overall. By \nMonday, the Western Atlantic ridge flattens and retrogrades \nfurther westward across the Northern Gulf Coast. At the same time,\nit also shows the upper shortwave absorbed back into the longwave\ntrough with just continued summertime diurnally enhanced activity\nexpected through the remainder of the week.\n\nAs for temperatures, readings vary only a degree or so here and \nthere over the next week with overall moisture remaining up. This \nwill result in max afternoon heat indices perhaps a couple of \ndegrees higher in spots. However, guidance continues to keep us \nbelow heat advisory criteria for now. \n\n08",
